In the previous discussion, we encountered a problem which requires arguing that a particular function is degree one in a spinor \pi^{\dot{\alpha}} when defined at a point in complexified Minkowski space M_C (\pi is a primed spinor field over M_C).  Let us try to prove this result.  The spinor function \lambda^{\alpha}(\pi^{\dot{ eta}}) must be homogeneous in \pi, and so takes the form  \lamda^{\alpha}(\pi) = \Phi^{\alpha}_{\dot{\alpha}\dot{ eta}\ldots}\pi^{\dot{\alpha}}\pi^{\dot{ eta}}\ldots.  It also satisfes \lambda^\alpha\pi^{\dot{\alpha}}
abla_{\alpha\dot{\alpha}}\lambda^ eta = 0, where 
abla is a connection that is not necessarily linear (this is part of what we are trying to prove).  So the LHS of the above equation should be given by \lambda^\alpha\pi^{\dot{\alpha}}
abla_{\alpha\dot{\alpha}}\lambda^ eta = \lambda^\alpha\pi^{\dot{\alpha}}(\partial_{\alpha\dot{\alpha}} + A_{\alpha\dot{\alpha}}(\pi))\lambda^ eta, where A can depend on \pi as well (written explicitly).  Is there any way to argue that \lambda or A are homogeneous of degree one in \pi?
